    "q-85424": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85424",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $591\\,030\\,47◻$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $2$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $4$",
      "worked_solution": "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n5+9+1+0+3+0+4+7+◻=29+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $2$ into the blank, we get $591\\,030\\,472,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $29+2=31,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $5$ into the blank, we get $591\\,030\\,475,$ which is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $4$ into the blank, we get $591\\,030\\,474,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $29+4=33,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
