    "q-85422": {
      "dom_id": "step-q85422",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $114\\,040\\,32◻$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $6$",
      "worked_solution": "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n1+1+4+0+4+0+3+2+◻=15+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $0$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,320,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $15+0=15,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $5$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,325,$ which is not divisible by $2.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $6$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,326,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $15+6=21,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
